// What the process was started with: its arguments, and its environment.
//
// Section 8 said what was missing and why it mattered: "there is no spelling
// for the arguments a process was started with and none for its environment,
// so the only program that can be written is one that computes what it was
// going to compute anyway". This is the way in. `panic.ft` and `stop` are the
// way out.
//
// **Why it is not `main`'s parameters.** A `main` that took its arguments
// would answer half of it -- an environment is a lookup and not a list -- and
// it would make `main` two functions where section 1 says a program has one
// beginning, "taking nothing". So both halves are read the way everything else
// outside the language is read: one routine per question, each a `%symbol`
// into the runtime, exactly as `fmt` and `mem` are. `runtime/src/env.rs` is
// the other half of this file.
//
// **What a `str` here is.** The bytes the process was started with, and not
// text: an argument may be anything a shell can pass and this language's `str`
// is bytes with a length, so nothing here checks or converts. Reading one is
// the same as reading any other `str`.
//
// **How long one is good for.** As long as the program: an argument names a
// piece of what the kernel handed over, and a variable names a copy nothing
// takes back. Neither is the collector's and neither has to be kept alive by
// holding it.

// ---- The arguments ---------------------------------------------------------

// How many there are, the program's own name among them -- so a program run
// with no arguments answers one, as a C `main` counts them.
%symbol("__rt_arg_count")
pub fn count(): i64

// The i'th of them, counting the name as the nought'th. An `i` outside them is
// the empty string rather than a refusal: what a program does about an
// argument it did not get is the program's, and there is no way to fail here
// that a caller could not have asked about first.
%symbol("__rt_arg")
pub fn arg(i: i64): str

// ---- The environment -------------------------------------------------------

// What it holds under that name, or nothing where it holds none.
//
// `get` and not `var`, which is a keyword: a binding is introduced with it,
// so no declaration may be called one.
//
// The empty string for a name that is not there *and* for one set to nothing.
// `has` below is what tells the two apart, and there are two routines rather
// than an `Option` for a plain reason: an `Option<str>` is an enum this
// language declares, and a `%symbol` fn gives back what the runtime can build.
%symbol("__rt_var")
pub fn get(name: str): str

// Whether it holds one at all, which is the question the empty string cannot
// answer.
%symbol("__rt_has_var")
pub fn has(name: str): bool
